    {
      "dom_id": "question-213057",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${15}^{4}≡$",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}=(3\\cdot 5)^{4}={3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 7+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{15}^{4} & ≡{3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\cdot 4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 7+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}≡1\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
