      "name": null,
      "problem": "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${20}^{4}≡$",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}=(4\\cdot 5)^{4}={4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ {5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 6+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{20}^{4} & ≡{4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}≡4\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$",
