    {
      "dom_id": "question-213048",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${6}^{6}≡$",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{6}^{6}={6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 8+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{6} & ≡{6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 8+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{6}^{6}≡0\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
