      "dom_id": "question-213071",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n${8}^{6}\\text{ mod }9=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}={8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2} & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 9+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{6} & ≡{8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}\\,\\,\\text{mod}\\,9=1.\n$$",
