      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${28}^{18}?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{28}^{18}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & ≡20+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{2} & ≡{8}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {28}^{6} & ≡{({28}^{2})}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{4}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${28}^{18}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{28}^{18}={({28}^{6})}^{3}\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{18} & ≡{({28}^{6})}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(4)^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{28}^{18}\\text{ mod }10=4\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${28}^{18}$ is $4.$",
