      "dom_id": "question-213184",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${12}^{17}?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{12}^{17}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & ≡10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{4} & ≡{2}^{4}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {12}^{8} & ≡{6}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡36\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${12}^{17}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{12}^{17}={({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{17} & ≡{({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(6)^{2}\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡72\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{17}\\text{ mod }10=2\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${12}^{17}$ is $2.$",
