    {
      "dom_id": "step-154628",
      "name": "Example: \n                    Computing a Final Digit",
      "problem": "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $17^{13}?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $a\\equiv b\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c\\equiv d\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac\\equiv bd  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a^k \\equiv b^k  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17 & ≡10+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{2} & ≡{7}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡49\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {17}^{4} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose $17^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n17^{13} =  \\left(17^4\\right)^3\\cdot 17\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{13} & ≡{({17}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 17\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{3}\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10 = 7\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of $17^{13}$ is $7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
