      ],
      "extraction_error_parts": {},
      "assets": [],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118157/observations/lesson-step-154615-9b46f094ecc50d72ef6b.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118157/observations/lesson-step-154615-9b46f094ecc50d72ef6b.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/2674",
      "captured_at": 1791725084.7069528,
      "math_academy_id": 9017,
      "title": "Introduction",
      "content": "Let's consider the modular congruence\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nOne useful property of congruences is that we can *multiply* both sides by an *integer*, similar to regular equations.\n\nFor example, consider the true statement\n\n$$\n7\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by (for example) $9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot 7 & ≡9\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 63 & ≡18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $63$ and $18$ both have a remainder of $3$ when divided by $5.$\n\nIn general, the **scalar multiplication property** of congruences states that for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z},$\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn).\n$$"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-154619",
      "name": "The Multiplicative Property",
      "problem": "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how.",
      "worked_solution": "",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": null,
      "proof_feedback": null,
