      "assets": [],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118157/observations/lesson-step-154619-d463ccf6f9ad01ca4674.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118157/observations/lesson-step-154619-d463ccf6f9ad01ca4674.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/2674",
      "captured_at": 1791725086.1043653,
      "math_academy_id": 9040,
      "title": "The Multiplicative Property",
      "content": "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how."
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-154627",
      "name": "The Exponentiation Property",
      "problem": "The multiplicative property states that given $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c \\equiv d \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ we obtain $ac \\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).$ By taking the same congruence twice, we obtain \n\n$$\na^2 \\equiv b^2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\na^k \\equiv b^k  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nfor any *positive* integer $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
